---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [["Which of the three is most important?", "DMARC, because it provides enforcement and reporting. But DMARC requires SPF or DKIM to pass first, so all three are needed for a complete setup."], ["Do I need all three for email deliverability?", "Gmail and Microsoft recommend all three for bulk senders. Two (SPF + DKIM) provide redundancy; DMARC adds enforcement."]];

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: "DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM",
  description: "DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM: how all three work together. SPF authorizes IPs, DKIM signs content, DMARC sets policy.",
  inLanguage: 'en',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://promptdns.com/favicon.svg' } },
  datePublished: '2026-10-27',
  dateModified: '2026-10-27',
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Which of the three is most important?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "DMARC, because it provides enforcement and reporting. But DMARC requires SPF or DKIM to pass first, so all three are needed for a complete setup." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Do I need all three for email deliverability?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Gmail and Microsoft recommend all three for bulk senders. Two (SPF + DKIM) provide redundancy; DMARC adds enforcement." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Which protocol should I deploy first?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "SPF first as the cheapest baseline, DKIM second because it survives forwarding, then DMARC to enforce and report. That order is the standard rollout." } }
  ],
};

const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Email Authentication', item: 'https://promptdns.com/email-authentication/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: "DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM" },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM: How All Three Work Together for Email Authentication"
  description="DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM: how all three work together. SPF authorizes IPs, DKIM signs content, DMARC sets policy."
  schema={[article, faqSchema, breadcrumbs]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-dim">DMARC vs SPF vs DKIM: how all three work together. SPF authorizes IPs, DKIM signs content, DMARC sets policy.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class='pdns-table' style='width:100%'><thead><tr><th class='whitespace-nowrap'></th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>SPF</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>DKIM</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>DMARC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class='pd-mono'>RFC</td><td>7208</td><td>6376</td><td>7489</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>What it does</td><td>Authorizes sending IPs</td><td>Signs message content</td><td>Sets policy + reports</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>DNS record</td><td>TXT at root (v=spf1)</td><td>TXT at selector._domainkey</td><td>TXT at _dmarc</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Survives forwarding</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Depends on which protocol aligns</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Provides reports</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (aggregate via rua=)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Stops spoofing alone?</td><td>Partially (IP-based)</td><td>Partially (crypto-based)</td><td>Yes (enforcement layer)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Works without the others?</td><td>Yes (but weak alone)</td><td>Yes (but weak alone)</td><td>No (needs SPF or DKIM)</td></tr></tbody></table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      <h2>Technical details</h2>
      <p class='mt-3'>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC are three complementary email authentication protocols that work together: SPF (RFC 7208) authorizes sending IP addresses, DKIM (RFC 6376) cryptographically signs message content, and DMARC (RFC 7489) defines the enforcement policy and reporting mechanism.</p><p class='mt-3'>DMARC requires at least one of SPF or DKIM to pass AND align with the visible From header domain; using both provides redundancy because SPF fails on forwarding while DKIM survives it.</p><p class='mt-3'>SPF and DKIM without DMARC provide authentication signals but no enforcement: receiving servers have no policy telling them what to do with messages that fail authentication.</p>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">The most resilient email authentication setup I have implemented uses all three: SPF as the baseline, DKIM as the forward-safe layer, and DMARC as the enforcement and reporting layer. Any two passing is sufficient for DMARC alignment, but all three passing is the goal.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which of the three is most important?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DMARC, because it provides enforcement and reporting. But DMARC requires SPF or DKIM to pass first, so all three are needed for a complete setup.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need all three for email deliverability?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Gmail and Microsoft recommend all three for bulk senders. Two (SPF + DKIM) provide redundancy; DMARC adds enforcement.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which protocol should I deploy first?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">SPF first as the cheapest baseline, DKIM second because it survives forwarding, then DMARC to enforce and report. That order is the standard rollout.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Check your domain with our free tools:
        <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF checker</a>,
        <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC checker</a>,
        <a href="/dkim-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DKIM checker</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
